{"question":"How can I export a class for the client in Kotlin/JS?","candidates":[{"anchor":"jsexport-annotation","start":3701,"end":4560,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"3e36da4b83ae9f33e669108aeee6a8671d7327481edcc5e401cf3f55d2ed1476","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"@JsExport annotation

By applying the @JsExport annotation to a top-level declaration (like a class, interface, or function), you can make Kotlin declarations available from JavaScript or TypeScript. The annotation exports all nested declarations with the name given in Kotlin.

For example, here's how you can export a Kotlin interface with a nested class and a named companion object:

@JsExport
interface Identity {
     class Metadata(val tag: String)

    companion object Registry {
        val defaultTag = "GUEST"
    }
}

Currently, the @JsExport annotation is the only way to make your functions visible from Kotlin.

The @JsExport annotation is also available:

In common code in multiplatform projects. It only has an effect when compiling for the JavaScript target and allows you to also export Kotlin declarations that are not platform-specific.","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsExport annotation"],"line_start":79,"line_end":98,"score":0.7205125093460083,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"export-value-classes","start":4801,"end":5522,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"f60ee257bc68fbb1db0cc9c8e023954bbaf0c921c300e62db8d26acce57e3575","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Export value classes

You can export Kotlin's inline value classes as regular TypeScript classes.

To export a value class, mark it with the @JsExport annotation on the Kotlin side:

// Kotlin
@JsExport
@JvmInline
value class Email(val address: String) {
    init { require(address.contains("@")) { "Invalid email" } }
}

@JsExport
class AuthService {
    suspend fun login(email: Email): String = ...
}

On the TypeScript side, it looks like a regular class:

// TypeScript
import { AuthService, Email } from "..."
const auth = new AuthService();

console.log(await auth.login(new Email("jane@example.com"))); 
// "Welcome, jane@example.com!"
console.log(await auth.login(new Email("not-an-email"))); 
// "Invalid email"","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsExport annotation > Export value classes"],"line_start":104,"line_end":131,"score":0.7133346796035767,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"support-for-value-class-export-to-javascript-typescript","start":27305,"end":28187,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common","jvm","android","native","js","wasm"],"id":"90202937a40e716dc00130a3514a613a75c38c679de5f9df1f1a999fc4288421","document_id":"whatsnew24","strategy":"semantic","text":"Support for value class export to JavaScript/TypeScript

Previously, only regular Kotlin classes could be exported to JavaScript/TypeScript. Kotlin 2.4.0 lifts that limitation. You can now export Kotlin's inline value classes as regular TypeScript classes.

To export a value class, mark it with the @JsExport annotation on the Kotlin side:

// Kotlin
@JsExport
@JvmInline
value class Email(val address: String) {
    init { require(address.contains("@")) { "Invalid email" } }
}

@JsExport
class AuthService {
    suspend fun login(email: Email): String = ...
}

From the TypeScript side, it looks like a regular class:

// TypeScript
import { AuthService, Email } from "..."
const auth = new AuthService();

console.log(await auth.login(new Email("jane@example.com"))); 
// "Welcome, jane@example.com!"
console.log(await auth.login(new Email("not-an-email"))); 
// "Invalid email"","title":"What's new in Kotlin 2.4.0","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew24.html","sections":["What's new in Kotlin 2.4.0 > Kotlin/JS > Support for value class export to JavaScript/TypeScript"],"line_start":680,"line_end":707,"score":0.6234996318817139,"release_version":"2.4.0","availability":"See individual feature status in source; release version is not a snippet compatibility guarantee."},{"anchor":"extend-javascript-classes","start":3904,"end":4579,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"c2d544ee48ba73d6d53d251f08275b18ccadeb9462ec7bb9811510eb067f9e77","document_id":"js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Extend JavaScript classes

You can easily extend JavaScript classes as if they were Kotlin classes. Just define an external open class and extend it by a non- external class. For example:

open external class Foo {
    open fun run()
    fun stop()
}

class Bar : Foo() {
    override fun run() {
        window.alert("Running!")
    }

    fun restart() {
        window.alert("Restarting")
    }
}

There are some limitations:

When a function of an external base class is overloaded by signature, you can't override it in a derived class.

You can't override a function including parameters with default values.

Non-external classes can't be extended by external classes.","title":"Use JavaScript code from Kotlin","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-interop.html","sections":["Use JavaScript code from Kotlin > external modifier > Extend JavaScript classes"],"line_start":99,"line_end":124,"score":0.6184996366500854,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"jsnoruntime-annotation","start":6433,"end":7105,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"a8d4728e1515d6b36cad884fad2c967233979a354b6dfee265f2bde2ee394f02","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"@JsNoRuntime annotation

You can export Kotlin interfaces to JavaScript/TypeScript with the @JsNoRuntime annotation. It allows for direct mapping to regular TypeScript interfaces.

To export a Kotlin interface, for example, from a Kotlin Multiplatform project:

Annotate the Kotlin interface with @JsNoRuntime in common code:

// commonMain
import kotlin.js.JsNoRuntime

@JsNoRuntime
expect interface DataProcessor {
    fun process(data: String): Int 
}

Provide the actual implementation with @JsNoRuntime in your JS-specific source code:

// jsMain
import kotlin.js.JsNoRuntime

@JsNoRuntime
actual interface DataProcessor {
    actual fun process(data: String): Int
} ","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsNoRuntime annotation"],"line_start":170,"line_end":194,"score":0.6128913164138794,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"}]}

{"question":"How can I export a class for the client in Kotlin/JS?","candidates":[{"anchor":"declare-static-members-of-a-class","start":2600,"end":3294,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"060b1716cf02e76f9cf790cff97af5e52be67ec32a4a2ead8cf56a9c78ab2117","document_id":"js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Declare (static) members of a class

In JavaScript, you can define members either on a prototype or a class itself:

function MyClass() { ... }
MyClass.sharedMember = function() { /* implementation */ };
MyClass.prototype.ownMember = function() { /* implementation */ };

There is no such syntax in Kotlin. However, in Kotlin we have companion objects. Kotlin treats companion objects of external classes in a special way: instead of expecting an object, it assumes members of companion objects to be members of the class itself. MyClass from the example above can be described as follows:

external class MyClass {
    companion object {
        fun sharedMember()
    }

    fun ownMember()
}","title":"Use JavaScript code from Kotlin","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-interop.html","sections":["Use JavaScript code from Kotlin > external modifier > Declare (static) members of a class"],"line_start":69,"line_end":85,"score":0.598635733127594,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"jsexport-annotation","start":4562,"end":4799,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"f3b5957a3b87cac3b3102b6d12df9a4b744b31e0a9fb27b237066cfd984f174c","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Together with the @JsName annotation to specify the names for the generated and exported functions. This helps to resolve ambiguities in exports (like overloads for functions with the same name).

At the file level using @file:JsExport .","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsExport annotation"],"line_start":100,"line_end":102,"score":0.5964491367340088,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"support-for-javascript-default-exports","start":23023,"end":23905,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common","jvm","android","native","js","wasm"],"id":"d7cc1096c9a709fbd88dc299c92a26d0462a72c986688d42c6f608c97081a277","document_id":"whatsnew23","strategy":"semantic","text":"Support for JavaScript default exports

Previously, Kotlin/JS could not generate JavaScript's default exports from Kotlin code. Instead, Kotlin/JS only generated named exports, for example:

export { SomeDeclaration };

If you required a default export, you had to use workarounds inside the compiler, such as placing the @JsName annotation with default and a space as an argument:

@JsExport
@JsName("default ")
class SomeDeclaration

Kotlin/JS now supports default exports directly through a new annotation:

@JsExport.Default

When you apply this annotation to a Kotlin declaration (class, object, function, or property), the generated JavaScript automatically includes an export default statement for ES modules:

export default HelloWorker;

For module systems different from ES modules, the new @JsExport.Default annotation works similarly to the regular @JsExport annotation.","title":"What's new in Kotlin 2.3.0","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew23.html","sections":["What's new in Kotlin 2.3.0 > Kotlin/JS > Support for JavaScript default exports"],"line_start":540,"line_end":560,"score":0.5955455303192139,"release_version":"2.3.0","availability":"See individual feature status in source; release version is not a snippet compatibility guarantee."},{"anchor":"jsname-annotation","start":2209,"end":2994,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"269e7ebeee6576296876fdccd3102addd0a7821ca8b7118294ac4894dcd8cf48","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"@JsName annotation

In some cases (for example, to support overloads), the Kotlin compiler mangles the names of generated functions and attributes in JavaScript code. To control the generated names, you can use the @JsName annotation:

// Module 'kjs'
class Person(val name: String) {
    fun hello() {
        println("Hello $name!")
    }

    @JsName("helloWithGreeting")
    fun hello(greeting: String) {
        println("$greeting $name!")
    }
}

Now you can use this class from JavaScript in the following way:

// If necessary, import 'kjs' according to chosen module system
var person = new kjs.Person("Dmitry");   // refers to module 'kjs'
person.hello();                          // prints "Hello Dmitry!"
person.helloWithGreeting("Servus");      // prints "Servus Dmitry!"","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsName annotation"],"line_start":43,"line_end":64,"score":0.5895646810531616,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"new-export-of-suspend-function-with-jsexport","start":17956,"end":18556,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common","jvm","android","native","js","wasm"],"id":"94fdd002f4c99e464f1107a787fafaca95f0ad9a490bfdc97254f3295de9e787","document_id":"whatsnew23","strategy":"semantic","text":"To enable this feature, add the following compiler option to your build.gradle.kts file:

kotlin {
    compilerOptions {
        freeCompilerArgs.add("-Xenable-suspend-function-exporting")
    }
}

Once enabled, classes and functions marked with the @JsExport annotation can include suspend functions without additional wrappers.

They can be consumed as regular JavaScript async functions and can be overridden as an async function, as well:

@JsExport
open class Foo {
    suspend fun foo() = "Foo"
}

class Bar extends Foo {
    override async foo(): Promise<string> {
        return "Bar"
    }
}","title":"What's new in Kotlin 2.3.0","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew23.html","sections":["What's new in Kotlin 2.3.0 > Kotlin/JS > New export of suspend function with JsExport"],"line_start":404,"line_end":425,"score":0.5823782086372375,"release_version":"2.3.0","availability":"See individual feature status in source; release version is not a snippet compatibility guarantee."}]}

