This change is enabled by default.","title":"What's new in Kotlin 2.2.0","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew22.html","sections":["What's new in Kotlin 2.2.0 > Kotlin/JS > Support for @JsExport in multiplatform expect declarations"],"line_start":743,"line_end":747,"score":0.5120084285736084,"release_version":"2.2.0","availability":"See individual feature status in source; release version is not a snippet compatibility guarantee."},{"anchor":"export-value-classes","start":4801,"end":5522,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"f60ee257bc68fbb1db0cc9c8e023954bbaf0c921c300e62db8d26acce57e3575","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Export value classes

You can export Kotlin's inline value classes as regular TypeScript classes.

To export a value class, mark it with the @JsExport annotation on the Kotlin side:

// Kotlin
@JsExport
@JvmInline
value class Email(val address: String) {
    init { require(address.contains("@")) { "Invalid email" } }
}

@JsExport
class AuthService {
    suspend fun login(email: Email): String = ...
}

On the TypeScript side, it looks like a regular class:

// TypeScript
import { AuthService, Email } from "..."
const auth = new AuthService();

console.log(await auth.login(new Email("jane@example.com"))); 
// "Welcome, jane@example.com!"
console.log(await auth.login(new Email("not-an-email"))); 
// "Invalid email"","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsExport annotation > Export value classes"],"line_start":104,"line_end":131,"score":0.49688392877578735,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"declare-parameters-with-default-values","start":3296,"end":3902,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"712359572c81b236f5c6335f575dd71d78b687a5a7fae28ff9f33488260e6d33","document_id":"js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Declare parameters with default values

If you are writing an external declaration for a JavaScript function which has a parameter with a default value, use definedExternally . This delegates the generation of the default values to the JavaScript function itself:

external fun myFunWithOptionalArgs(
    x: Int,
    y: String = definedExternally,
    z: String = definedExternally
)

With this external declaration, you can call myFunWithOptionalArgs with one required argument and two optional arguments, where the default values are calculated by the JavaScript implementation of myFunWithOptionalArgs .","title":"Use JavaScript code from Kotlin","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-interop.html","sections":["Use JavaScript code from Kotlin > external modifier > Declare parameters with default values"],"line_start":87,"line_end":97,"score":0.4924303889274597,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"external-modifier","start":1592,"end":2598,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"ea1bb45f19d675790de3284d7eebb628497bbd4954c59494aa2d1330f9aadb36","document_id":"js-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"external modifier

To tell Kotlin that a certain declaration is written in pure JavaScript, you should mark it with the external modifier. When the compiler sees such a declaration, it assumes that the implementation for the corresponding class, function or property is provided externally (by the developer or via an npm dependency ), and therefore does not try to generate any JavaScript code from the declaration. This is also why external declarations can't have a body. For example:

external fun alert(message: Any?): Unit

external class Node {
    val firstChild: Node

    fun append(child: Node): Node

    fun removeChild(child: Node): Node

    // etc
}

external val window: Window

Note that the external modifier is inherited by nested declarations. This is why in the example Node class, there is no external modifier before member functions and properties.

The external modifier is only allowed on package-level declarations. You can't declare an external member of a non- external class.","title":"Use JavaScript code from Kotlin","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-interop.html","sections":["Use JavaScript code from Kotlin > external modifier"],"line_start":47,"line_end":67,"score":0.4809233546257019,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"}]}

