If you have explicitly set your module kind to be plain , Kotlin creates an object that contains all Kotlin declarations from the current module. This is done to prevent spoiling the global object. This means that for a module myModule , all declarations are available to JavaScript via the myModule object. For example:

fun foo() = "Hello"

This function can be called from JavaScript like this:

alert(myModule.foo());

Calling the function directly like this is not applicable when you compile your Kotlin module to JavaScript modules like UMD (the default setting for both browser and nodejs targets), ESM , CommonJS , or AMD . In these cases, your declarations are exposed according to the chosen JavaScript module system. For example, when using UMD, ESM, or CommonJS, your call site would look like this:

alert(require('myModule').foo());","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Isolating declarations in a separate JavaScript object in plain mode"],"line_start":5,"line_end":17,"score":0.47140148282051086,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"function-names","start":6649,"end":6850,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common"],"id":"a2da8aabe89d0fe4b328ccdeb37d0834caf57846c4678b31c097fc0d360baa16","document_id":"coding-conventions","strategy":"semantic","text":"Function names

Names of functions, properties, and local variables start with a lowercase letter and use camel case without underscores:

fun processDeclarations() { /*...*/ }
var declarationCount = 1","title":"Coding conventions","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coding-conventions.html","sections":["Coding conventions > Naming rules > Function names"],"line_start":117,"line_end":122,"score":0.4635162949562073,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"use-long-in-exported-declarations","start":9908,"end":10498,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"2acd20adaf8e5b999755359c377e388646b2d7453c4c2be926528825ccd52fc3","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Use Long in exported declarations

Since Kotlin's Long type can compile to JavaScript's BigInt type, Kotlin/JS supports exporting Long values to JavaScript.

To enable this feature:

Allow exporting Long in Kotlin/JS. Add the following compiler option to the freeCompilerArgs attribute in your build.gradle(.kts) file:

// build.gradle.kts
kotlin {
   js {
       ...
       compilerOptions { 
           freeCompilerArgs.add("-XXLanguage:+JsAllowLongInExportedDeclarations")
       }
   }
}

Enable the BigInt type. See how to enable it in Use BigInt type to represent Kotlin's Long type .","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > Use BigInt type to represent Kotlin's Long type > Use Long in exported declarations"],"line_start":264,"line_end":282,"score":0.463117778301239,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"property-names","start":7712,"end":8376,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common"],"id":"54c225497944b0ee3c77f7504844e98a18dda271d4450c0923a4a977726e03fb","document_id":"coding-conventions","strategy":"semantic","text":"Property names

Names of constants (properties marked with const , or top-level or object val properties with no custom get function that hold deeply immutable data) should use all uppercase, underscore-separated names following the screaming snake case convention:

const val MAX_COUNT = 8
val USER_NAME_FIELD = "UserName"

Names of top-level or object properties which hold objects with behavior or mutable data should use camel case names:

val mutableCollection: MutableSet<String> = HashSet()

Names of properties holding references to singleton objects can use the same naming style as object declarations:

val PersonComparator: Comparator<Person> = /*...*/","title":"Coding conventions","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coding-conventions.html","sections":["Coding conventions > Naming rules > Property names"],"line_start":150,"line_end":163,"score":0.4577469527721405,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"}]}

{"question":"How should I give an exported declaration a predictable JavaScript name?","candidates":[{"anchor":"jsnoruntime-annotation","start":6433,"end":7105,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"a8d4728e1515d6b36cad884fad2c967233979a354b6dfee265f2bde2ee394f02","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"@JsNoRuntime annotation

You can export Kotlin interfaces to JavaScript/TypeScript with the @JsNoRuntime annotation. It allows for direct mapping to regular TypeScript interfaces.

To export a Kotlin interface, for example, from a Kotlin Multiplatform project:

Annotate the Kotlin interface with @JsNoRuntime in common code:

// commonMain
import kotlin.js.JsNoRuntime

@JsNoRuntime
expect interface DataProcessor {
    fun process(data: String): Int 
}

Provide the actual implementation with @JsNoRuntime in your JS-specific source code:

// jsMain
import kotlin.js.JsNoRuntime

@JsNoRuntime
actual interface DataProcessor {
    actual fun process(data: String): Int
} ","title":"Use Kotlin code from JavaScript","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/js-to-kotlin-interop.html","sections":["Use Kotlin code from JavaScript > Package structure > @JsNoRuntime annotation"],"line_start":170,"line_end":194,"score":0.4575476050376892,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"deprecation-of-default-exports","start":38878,"end":39681,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common","jvm","android","native","js","wasm"],"id":"dd184a70ba705fa911110bcd5213fc1b460ee2039c7496386590ee95f25a6e11","document_id":"whatsnew21","strategy":"semantic","text":"Deprecation of default exports

As part of the migration to named exports, an error was previously printed to the console when a default import was used for Kotlin/Wasm exports in JavaScript.

In 2.1.0, default imports have been completely removed to fully support named exports.

When coding in JavaScript for the Kotlin/Wasm target, you now need to use the corresponding named imports instead of default imports.

This change marks the last phase of a deprecation cycle to migrate to named exports:

In version 2.0.0: A warning message was printed to the console, explaining that exporting entities via default exports is deprecated.

In version 2.0.20: An error occurred, requesting the use of the corresponding named import.

In version 2.1.0: The use of default imports has been completely removed.","title":"What's new in Kotlin 2.1.0","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew21.html","sections":["What's new in Kotlin 2.1.0 > Kotlin/Wasm > Deprecation of default exports"],"line_start":808,"line_end":822,"score":0.4472963511943817,"release_version":"2.1.0","availability":"See individual feature status in source; release version is not a snippet compatibility guarantee."},{"anchor":"export-suspending-lambdas","start":5524,"end":6173,"split_code":false,"platforms":["js"],"id":"7d2f22f51777f8186e1ba44e3888b0f34d7422cabcbfa7b935265bc5b89125ca","document_id":"js-to-kotlin-interop","strategy":"semantic","text":"Export suspending lambdas

