When you use a type projection, such as out T or in T , the compiler internally represents the unknown concrete type as a captured type . A captured type is an unknown type with known upper and lower bounds.

Captured types are non-denotable, so you can't write them directly in Kotlin code. Instead, you can most frequently see captured types in compiler diagnostics, such as CapturedType(out X) . For example, the following type mismatch diagnostic contains a captured type:

val array: Array<out CharSequence> = arrayOf("str")
val item: Int = array.get(0)
// Initializer type mismatch: expected 'Int', actual 'CapturedType(out CharSequence)'","title":"Generics","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/generics.html","sections":["Generics: in, out, where > Type projections > Captured types"],"line_start":193,"line_end":201,"score":0.29797568917274475,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"named-arguments","start":26678,"end":26966,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common"],"id":"d7d9088ce64f4122f8676a457a7dc7671d4dd8bf6b7361506584be0b3af6f082","document_id":"coding-conventions","strategy":"semantic","text":"Named arguments

