Since extension and member function calls have the same notation, how does the compiler know which one to use? Extension functions are dispatched statically , meaning the compiler determines which function to call based on the receiver type at compile time. For example:

fun main() {
//sampleStart
    open class Shape
    class Rectangle: Shape()

    fun Shape.getName() = "Shape"
    fun Rectangle.getName() = "Rectangle"

    fun printClassName(shape: Shape) {
        println(shape.getName())
    }

    printClassName(Rectangle())
    // Shape
//sampleEnd
}","title":"Extensions","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/extensions.html","sections":["Extensions > Extension functions > Extension or member functions?"],"line_start":148,"line_end":167,"score":0.3480033576488495,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"anonymous-extension-functions","start":8326,"end":8978,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common"],"id":"8318a20f11f0f72b2840429c894277ce5b09870c57251035b33731f3d1e58b5f","document_id":"extensions","strategy":"semantic","text":"Anonymous extension functions

You can define extension functions without giving them a name. This is useful when you want to avoid cluttering the global namespace or when you need to pass some extension behavior as a parameter.

For example, suppose you want to extend a data class with a one-time function to calculate shipping, without giving it a name:

fun main() {
    //sampleStart
    data class Order(val weight: Double)
    val calculateShipping = fun Order.(rate: Double): Double = this.weight * rate

    val order = Order(2.5)
    val cost = order.calculateShipping(3.0)
    println("Shipping cost: $cost") 
    // Shipping cost: 7.5
}","title":"Extensions","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/extensions.html","sections":["Extensions > Extension functions > Anonymous extension functions"],"line_start":206,"line_end":221,"score":0.3478897511959076,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"changes-to-top-level-require-calls-in-jsfun-declarations","start":15435,"end":16149,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common","jvm","android","native","js","wasm"],"id":"006c215ad108eda433c3ffbdc5a2a39c2fdc8ee5f6f1697a477eb018b42b1732","document_id":"whatsnew2420","strategy":"semantic","text":"For dynamic module loading, use the import() expression instead. Add the /* webpackIgnore: true */ magic comment to prevent webpack from parsing the dynamic import:

@JsFun("""
    ((module) => () => module)(
        await import(/* webpackIgnore: true */ "module")
    )
""")
private external fun loadModuleDynamically(): JsAny?

You can also use the import() expression conditionally. For example, you can load a module only when running in Node.js:

@JsFun("""
    ((module) => () => module)(
        ((typeof process !== "undefined") && (process.release.name === "node"))
            ? await import(/* webpackIgnore: true */ "module")
            : null
    )
""")
private external fun loadNodeModule(): JsAny?","title":"What's new in Kotlin 2.4.20","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/whatsnew2420.html","sections":["What's new in Kotlin 2.4.20 > Kotlin/Wasm > Changes to top-level require() calls in @JsFun declarations"],"line_start":339,"line_end":357,"score":0.34518173336982727,"release_version":"2.4.20","availability":"See individual feature status in source; release version is not a snippet compatibility guarantee."}]}

