When a Kotlin function called from Swift/Objective-C code throws an exception which is an instance of one of the classes specified with @Throws or their subclasses, the exception is propagated as an NSError . Other Kotlin exceptions reaching Swift/Objective-C are considered unhandled and cause program termination.

suspend functions without @Throws propagate only CancellationException (as NSError ). Non- suspend functions without @Throws don't propagate Kotlin exceptions at all.

Note that the opposite reversed translation is not implemented yet: Swift/Objective-C error-throwing methods aren't imported to Kotlin as exception-throwing.","title":"Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/native-objc-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C > Mappings > Errors and exceptions"],"line_start":218,"line_end":228,"score":0.5950535535812378,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"new-export-of-suspend-function-with-jsexport","start":17332,"end":17954,"split_code":false,"platforms":["common","jvm","android","native","js","wasm"],"id":"72aac8f3d4897743b042472b067c4ba3f44643ff24a22122085cd8a0c18ec2c0","document_id":"whatsnew23","strategy":"semantic","text":"New export of suspend function with JsExport

