{
  "status": "answered",
  "answer": "Through the Objective-C export bridge, a Kotlin String is mapped to Objective-C NSString, and in Swift it is mapped to String [2]. When a Kotlin String is passed to Swift, it's first exported as an Objective-C object, and then the Swift compiler copies it one more time for a Swift conversion, which results in additional runtime overhead [1]. To avoid that, the client should access Kotlin strings in Swift directly as an Objective-C NSString instead [1]. Where Kotlin strings appear as values inside collections, the same mapping applies: string values are mapped to Swift's String (for example, a Kotlin Map<String, String> is implicitly converted to Swift's Dictionary and its string values are mapped to Swift's String), also incurring a performance cost; to avoid the conversion, the map can be explicitly cast to Objective-C's NSDictionary and values accessed as NSString instead [3].",
