Objective-C classes are imported into Kotlin with their original names. Protocols are imported as interfaces with a Protocol name suffix, for example, @protocol Foo -> interface FooProtocol . These classes and interfaces are placed into a package specified in build configuration ( platform.* packages for preconfigured system frameworks).

The names of Kotlin classes and interfaces are prefixed when imported to Objective-C. The prefix is derived from the framework name.

Objective-C does not support packages in a framework. If the Kotlin compiler finds Kotlin classes in the same framework which have the same name but different packages, it renames them. This algorithm is not stable yet and can change between Kotlin releases. To work around this, you can rename the conflicting Kotlin classes in the framework.","title":"Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C","source":"https://kotlinlang.org/docs/native-objc-interop.html","sections":["Interoperability with Swift/Objective-C > Mappings > Classes > Name translation"],"line_start":146,"line_end":152,"score":0.5790733098983765,"release_version":null,"availability":"unknown"},{"anchor":"current-limitations","start":3521,"end":4170,"split_code":false,"platforms":["native"],"id":"e94bb8275a3de4cb7e0e40ddc376d48cfe7754029d451aa38fbf4839645979e4","document_id":"native-swift-export","strategy":"semantic","text":"Current limitations

